{"text": "1.2. Investigations on the effect of regul:\n\nating\nsmoking on levels of indoor pollution\nand on the perception\nof health and comfort of office workers\n\n\u2018Taopor D. STERLING AND ELtA M, STERLING\n\netopUcTION\n\nSince the mid-1970s many investigations of\nbuilding associated epidemics of complaints\nand illnesses have been undertaken in North\nAmerica and Burope in response to occupants?\nhealth complaints. The majority of these inves-\ntigations coincide in time with a concerted\neffort 10 minimize, building energy use by\nreducing fresh air ventilation rates and\nincreasing the operational comfort ranges for\nacceptable temperature and humidity in air\nconditioned, mechanically ventilated build-\nings (1). Health complaints have ranged from\nheadache and eye irtitation to reproductive\nsystems and pregnancy problems. Almost all\nthese instances have involved new or refur-\nnithed buildings in which conditions of\nambient air are completely mechanically con-\ntrolled and lighting supplied by fluorescent\nlamps. Only ina few instances were complaints\nor illnesses traced back to specific causes such\nas carpet shampoo residues, fiberglass from\nducting systems, rock wool, and excessive,\nlevels of formaldehyde. But, specific causes for\n\nsymptoms were not determined for most inves:\ntigations.\n\u2018The term \u201cBuilding Wlness\u201d hat been used\nto refer to these epidemies of complaints from\nbuilding occupants about symptoms and dis-\ncomfort including headaches, burning eyes,\nitritation ofthe respiratory system, drowsiness,\nfatigue and general malaise experienced over\n\u2018an extended period of time with the cause\nremaining undetermined but suspected to be\nrelated to components of the building or air\nsupply system (2,3). In modern, energy-efi-\ncient offce buildings tobacco smoke, perhaps\nbecause ofits visibility, is frequently regarded\n12, important source of airborne particulates\n\u2018and gases It is thus only natural that a great\ndeal of attention has been concentrated on\nsmoking as a source of indoor airborne sub-\nstances and as a possible cause for Building\nMness. However, modern buildings tend 10\ngenerate a large variety of pollutants as well as\nto entrap large numbers of them penetrating\nfrom the outside. Evidence from many studies\nhas now demonstrtted that buildings indeed\n\u2018entrap and concentrate \u00ab large variety of pol-\n\nHASTER-SPP 001677", "words": [{"text": "1.2.", "x0": 173, "y0": 222, "x1": 201, "y1": 237, "conf": 85.0}, {"text": "Investigations", "x0": 214, "y0": 222, "x1": 337, "y1": 244, "conf": 95.0}, {"text": "on", "x0": 346, "y0": 229, "x1": 367, "y1": 240, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "the", "x0": 375, "y0": 225, "x1": 403, "y1": 240, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "effect", "x0": 411, "y0": 225, "x1": 458, "y1": 241, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "of", "x0": 466, "y0": 226, "x1": 485, "y1": 241, "conf": 93.0}, {"text": "regul:", "x0": 491, "y0": 227, "x1": 534, "y1": 247, "conf": 73.0}, {"text": "ating", "x0": 536, "y0": 227, "x1": 579, "y1": 247, "conf": 77.0}, {"text": "smoking", "x0": 204, "y0": 246, "x1": 280, "y1": 266, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "on", "x0": 287, "y0": 251, "x1": 308, "y1": 262, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "levels", "x0": 316, "y0": 247, "x1": 365, "y1": 263, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "of", "x0": 373, "y0": 247, "x1": 393, "y1": 263, "conf": 97.0}, {"text": "indoor", "x0": 398, "y0": 248, "x1": 457, "y1": 263, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "pollution", "x0": 464, "y0": 249, "x1": 544, "y1": 269, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "and", "x0": 276, "y0": 269, "x1": 307, "y1": 285, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "on", "x0": 315, "y0": 275, "x1": 337, "y1": 285, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "the", "x0": 345, "y0": 270, "x1": 372, "y1": 286, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "perception", "x0": 380, "y0": 272, "x1": 473, "y1": 292, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "of", "x0": 195, "y0": 291, "x1": 214, "y1": 306, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "health", "x0": 220, "y0": 291, "x1": 275, "y1": 307, "conf": 96.0}